{"kind":"assert","text":"On preventing multiple local servers against one repo path: the durable log is already safe; the live nexus is not, and it splits silently. Four verified facts. (1) Durable appends are genuinely compare-and-swap. kernel.go calls store.UpdateRef(ctx, ref, commit, head), which runs git update-ref \u003cref\u003e \u003cnew\u003e \u003cold\u003e; the old-value form fails if the ref moved, and the kernel retries up to 32 times, reporting the count as cas_retries. Atomicity belongs to git, not to the server process, so two servers — or a server plus a bare gs CLI invocation — appending concurrently is safe. Durable corruption is not the hazard. (2) The live nexus is entirely in-memory and its generation is random per process. nexus.go mints \"generation:\" + 16 random bytes at Hub construction and holds presence, convs, about, and participants as plain in-process maps. Two servers on one repo path therefore run two disjoint nexuses under two different generations. Clients attached to server A never see presence or ephemeral frames from clients on server B, each side believes it sees the whole room, and neither receives any signal that a split exists. This is the real hazard, and it is silent. (3) There is no lock of any kind: no flock, no lockfile, no already-running probe anywhere in the tree. Nothing but port contention prevents the second server. (4) Port contention does stop a same-port start, but reports it dishonestly. serveCommand prints \"gitseq workroom http://\u003caddr\u003e\" to stderr before calling ListenAndServe, so a failed bind still emits a confident ready banner followed by the bind error. I hit this accidentally minutes ago while restarting the workroom: my process printed the ready banner, then \"listen tcp 127.0.0.1:7777: bind: address already in use\", and exited. An operator reading only the banner would believe their server came up. So: yes, defend against it, but the thing to defend is the live split rather than the log, and the honest fix is probably a lockfile in the Git common directory, which would also behave correctly for linked worktrees. Fixing the banner ordering is independent and cheap."}